0: prefix:      'He found it a sore subject. Watson was a decent fellow and disposed to be fair-minded, but Henry Underwood was a red rag to him. The way in which the police force had been defied and outwitted in the former outbreak was not likely to soften their attitude toward the culprit in the present case. The hope of proving Henry guilty was evidently dear to the official heart, and Burton departed, feeling that there was no help to be looked for in that direction. The rigor of the law was all that the Underwood family could expect. It was evening before he found the time and opportunity to take his basket to the Red House. Mrs. Bussey did not appear. Instead, it was Leslie herself who admitted him, and conducted him to the surgery.\n\n"See what a bargain I have found," said Burton, displaying his purchase.\n\nThe doctor gave it a casual glance. "An Indian basket, isn\'t it? And not a very good one."\n\n"A very good--for my purpose. I wish I had another. Do you know any one in town who could weave one for 
0: me?"\n\n"No, I\'m afraid not." The doctor made an obvious effort to respond to his guest\'s trivial interests.\n\n"Are there any Indians living in or near town?"\n\n"No. They were all corralled on the Reservation years ago. There is a squaw who comes down from the Reservation to sell beadwork and things like that on the streets, but she is the only one I ever see nowadays."\n\n"Yes, I got this basket from her today. But I want a mate to it. Is there any one in town who can weave in the Indian fashion?"\n\n"I don\'t know of any one."\n\n"Would you know if there were any one? Excuse the persistence of a tourist and a faddist!"\n\nUnderwood aroused himself to a more genuine interest. "Why, if it is a matter that you have your heart set upon, I certainly should be glad to give you any information possible. But I don\'t believe there is any one in town who makes any attempt at that sort of work, or takes any interest in it. I should certainly know if any one made a profession of it, or even had a well-developed fa
0: d for it, to use your own word. Why? Is the basket rare?"\n\n"I have never seen that particular knot before. What\'s more, I didn\'t know that the mid-continent Indians did that sort of weaving'
0: 
0: megatron:    '. The one I know is an ancestral one."\n\n"Indeed, has anybody ever been able to find one? On the way up I was talking with one. My uncle, who was passing through the village, remarked that he had been here about a month, and that he had not noticed any change. \'Twas not the first time he saw me, though I was a little more careful last year. I knew I was to ask your uncle to marry me."\n\n"I see. So I have been able to find you," said Burton, with a quiet smile. "But I think the matter has been settled, and I was rather struck at the time."\n\n"Then you think it a great pity that you should be able to make any headway?"\n\n"Only a little. I know of one I met in the Southwest, and he had a tiff with him then. He went to a bar in the town, and got drunk and shot him over the head. I was fortunate enough to meet him at the bar, and I was not at all sure that he was not killing his wife."\n\n"Well, then, suppose you tell me the story of your discovery and my own surprise at the way you look at thin
0: gs. How did it strike you?"\n\n"Very well. This is the point I want to make. The discovery I made was that I had a friend who was the murderer of my wife. It was a plain murder. I was able to tell him that he was the murderer, and that he had a wife. That is why I brought you the basket."\n\n"Will you kindly give it to me?"\n\n"I will gladly. I will remember that I was a stranger. I have it before me in my own language, and I can read it. I will tell you the story of the murder, and we will see what you have to say about it."\n\n"Oh, I see. I\'m a fool. I had my suspicions, and I got the story from the girl, and I am sure that it will be a comfort to her to hear it. I am so sorry that my uncle did not kill his wife. But I am sure that I can do something to help him."\n\n"You are very kind. I\'ll be as honest as I can, if I can find out what the trouble was."\n\n"It is only that I can do that. When I heard the story that Henry Underwood was the murderer, I knew that I had a feeling that I could'
0: hf:          '. The one I know is an ancestral one."\n\n"Indeed, has anybody ever been able to find one? On the way up I should like to see one. Is there any one here in town who knows the name of that basket?"\n\n"No, I haven\'t seen any that I can think of. I don\'t know even the name of the village."\n\n"Then you mean you have one?"\n\n"Only one. I\'ve seen one. They are the kind of people I expect to find, but not one of them I would know. There is no one in the village. It\'s a shame they don\'t tell the name."\n\n"How long have you been out?"\n\n"About a month."\n\n"Did you ever know them?"\n\n"No, but I\'ve heard that they say there was some one in the village that was good to them."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"Your informant."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"An Indian boy."\n\n"Who is the boy?"\n\n"An Indian boy. He\'s been going to stay at our house all winter. The day after he came back, I was out, and I had a little basket. I wanted to have a good look at the place, but I couldn\'t say no. I suppose I must have done it, but I did
0: n\'t know who it was. I didn\'t see him for a week. I was at the village again, but didn\'t know who it was. I\'ve been here for a week, but I haven\'t seen him."\n\n"Is it the same boy every time I go there, or is it something different?"\n\n"Of course not. He is a good boy, and I don\'t know what he is. I have heard that there is a village somewhere in the world that is called him. I have had a friend there, and have heard of his name. He is a good boy, and I have seen him. I am his mother\'s sister."\n\n"Does she know?"\n\n"Of course she doesn\'t."\n\n"Did you see him?"\n\n"No, I didn\'t. I saw him this morning, but I\'m only a little boy, but I remember that he was here, and he wanted to know who he was."\n\n"He was here?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Did he come?"\n\n"Yes. He was to be a friend of mine."\n\n"Did he come?"\n\n"Who?"\n\n"The boy. He said he was the son of a good friend of mine."\n\n"Who is the boy?"\n\n"An Indian boy."\n\n"'
