0: megatron:    ' better than I do, they\'re sure to be after me for hours, sure," thought Crockett as he picked himself up and dusted off his horse.\n\nCHAPTER VIII.\nMARTIN POLLY.\n\nThe day for thinking of the matter was pleasant, and Crockett had made up his mind to return to his home as soon as he could, and take a short time to get his bearings.\n\nHe was more careful to keep out of the woods than he had been at the time of day, and, perhaps, to his own safety; for, if not, he thought he had been mistaken in his judgment.\n\nThe sun, at the end of a hundred and fifty minutes, had risen in a clear sky, and, as Crockett entered the shadow of the wood, he could see that it was growing dark. He had spent the first half hour hunting, and had not been able to get up enough to start for his home, when he heard a shout of Indians coming toward him. He hastened to the spot, and soon found three men, all dressed in the skins they had seen in the wood, standing with arms akimbo, and placing their bows and arrows in t
