0: hf:          ' better than I do, they\'ll never catch me," muttered Crockett, as he and his horse were walking briskly about the edge of the timber.\n\nSoon they were opposite the edge of the wood, and Crockett, who had been awakened by the noise, was kneeling beside the saddle, when the shout came from the Indians, and they wheeled about, as if they were making ready to go into camp.\n\n"I wonder what they are doing now," mused Crockett, "all these are running to the camp, and leaving, with their horses tied up, and their rifles stuck in the trees. There\'s not a dozen in the whole band."\n\nHe and the others had evidently come to the conclusion that the band of the tribe was still in the neighborhood, and that the news had not been received in vain. Then the savages, and at this time, were aware that the chief, who seemed to be guiding them, was watching the rear of the party, and Crockett, as if he had discovered a mistake, was not sorry that the chief should be watching him, and he would have sent a party
